   {
    "q": "Why is the RGB light system called additive?",
    "options": [
     "Because colours are added in layers of ink",
     "Because the three primaries together create all the hues in the spectrum",
     "Because it adds black to every colour",
     "Because it is used only for printing"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explain": "CMYK printing works the opposite way — inks subtract light.",
    "lesson": "graphic_design-02",
    "evidence": "This light system is called additive because the three primaries together create all the hues in the spectrum.",
    "origin": "authored"
   },
   {
    "q": "How many colour systems must designers be aware of, often in the same project?",
    "options": [
     "One — RGB",
     "Three — RGB, CMYK and HSV",
     "Four — one per printing ink",
     "Two — RGB and CMYK"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Screens use RGB light; print uses CMYK inks.",
    "lesson": "graphic_design-02",
    "evidence": "it is important to realize that there are two colour systems, and often a project needs to work in both.",
    "origin": "authored"
   },
   {
    "q": "What is emphasis in composition?",
    "options": [
     "Making a specific element gain the attention of the eye",
     "Using only one font",
     "Centring every element",
     "Repeating the same image"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Contrast in size, colour or position creates emphasis.",
    "lesson": "graphic_design-04",
    "evidence": "This is called emphasis and is achieved by making a specific element gain the attention of the eye.",
    "origin": "authored"
   },
   {
    "q": "When is a single-column grid an excellent approach?",
    "options": [
     "For complex newspapers",
     "For dashboards with many charts",
     "When the content is formatted in a simple manner",
     "Never — grids should always have several columns"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explain": "Choose the grid to suit the structure of the content.",
